    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d334">

                <pb facs="0503" n="423" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg423"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.114 Belgium/277: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Belgium</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Sussdorff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-07-07T17:00:00+01:00">July 7, 1936—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 7—1:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>47. Department’s 20, June 25, 5 p.m., and my 44, June 26, 7 p.m.<anchor n="25"
                        xml:id="d334-f1"/> Costermans has just informed me that the Belgian Ministry
                    of Finance has approved new regulations providing that shipment of alcohol from
                    Belgium shall henceforth be restricted to vessels of regular route and itinerary
                    and minimum tonnage of 3000 tons net and to barrels containing 100 liters which
                    he declares will weigh about 200 kilograms and will be too heavy to transship at
                    sea without the aid of a crane. Costermans stated that a decree will be signed
                    by the Minister of Finance in a few days and that the new regulations will then
                    be put into effect as an administrative measure.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sussdorff</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
